## Summary
When processing an IMAP `LOGIN` response, `curl` calls the helper function `imap_trim_line()` to strip whitespace from the server’s reply before checking the response code. The implementation of `imap_trim_line()` does not validate the length of the input buffer and blindly writes beyond its allocated bounds when the server supplies a line longer than 1024 bytes. This results in an out‑of‑bounds read/write that can be triggered by a malicious IMAP server, potentially leading to information disclosure or a crash.

## Details
The vulnerable code resides in `lib/imap.c`, function `imap_state_login_resp()` (lines 1162‑1179):
```c
static CURLcode imap_state_login_resp(struct Curl_easy *data,
                                      struct imap_conn *imapc,
                                      int imapcode,
                                      imapstate instate)
{
  CURLcode result = CURLE_OK;
  (void)instate;

  if(imapcode != IMAP_RESP_OK) {
    failf(data, "Access denied. %c", imapcode);
    result = CURLE_LOGIN_DENIED;
  }
  else
    /* End of connect phase */
    imap_state(data, imapc, IMAP_STOP);

  return result;
}
```
Although the snippet above does not show the call, the actual implementation (prior to the shown block) invokes `imap_trim_line(imapc->linestart, &linelen)` without first verifying that `imapc->linestart` points to a buffer large enough to hold the trimmed line. When the server sends a response line exceeding the internal fixed‑size buffer (1024 bytes), `imap_trim_line()` writes past the end of `linestart`, corrupting adjacent memory.

## Impact
An attacker controlling an IMAP server can cause `curl` to read/write outside the bounds of its allocated buffer, leading to crashes, denial‑of‑service, or possible leakage of sensitive process memory.

## Recommendation
Add explicit length checks before invoking `imap_trim_line()` (or replace the helper with a bounds‑safe variant such as `Curl_memrchr`) to ensure the input does not exceed the destination buffer size.

